# Unbound Technology — English localization

itemGroup.unboundtech=Unbound Technology

tile.unboundtech.thaum_generator.name=Thaumic Alternator
tile.unboundtech.aetheric_engine.name=Aetheric Engine

tc.research_category.UNBOUNDTECH=Industrial Magic

tc.research_name.THAUM_IC2_INTRO=Industrial Magic
tc.research_text.THAUM_IC2_INTRO=Two languages of one energy
unboundtech.research_page.THAUM_IC2_INTRO.1=Scanning the machine, you expected emptiness — crude iron, a dead mechanism. Instead the thaumometer trembled: power flows within. Not Vis — something else, steady and indifferent, like a river without a source. The engineers call it "EU".<BR><BR>The discovery is unsettling and alluring at once. Two forces drive this world, and until now you commanded only one.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUM_IC2_INTRO.2=A direct connection is impossible: Vis is alive and capricious, EU is deathly stable. A wire soldered to a crystal yields nothing but sparks and a burnt smell.<BR><BR>But where two languages meet, a translator can always be found. Master both forces — and the bridge will be built.

tc.research_name.LORE_EU_AND_VIS=EU and Vis
tc.research_text.LORE_EU_AND_VIS=Why they cannot connect directly
unboundtech.research_page.LORE_EU_AND_VIS.1=EU is discrete, countable, obedient. Vis is continuous, aspected, wilful. One flows through wires, the other through meanings.<BR><BR>Every translator takes a fee. Experiments show that any conversion cycle scatters at least three quarters of the power. A perpetual engine on the border of magic and machinery is impossible — and that is probably for the best.

tc.research_name.LORE_ELAN_VITAL=Elan Vital
tc.research_text.LORE_ELAN_VITAL=Why magic destabilizes circuitry
unboundtech.research_page.LORE_ELAN_VITAL.1=A saturated aura meddles with delicate circuits: signals drift, contacts spark, machines grow "moody". Engineers blame the humidity. You know the truth.<BR><BR>The aura is a living medium, and electricity is foreign to it. Until you find a way to insulate one from the other, keep fine electronics away from nodes of power.

tc.research_name.THAUM_ORE_MACERATION=Macerating Magical Ores
tc.research_text.THAUM_ORE_MACERATION=The macerator versus cinnabar
unboundtech.research_page.THAUM_ORE_MACERATION.1=The macerator holds no reverence for magic: to it, cinnabar is merely soft stone. But the result speaks for itself — one ore yields twice the quicksilver of smelting.<BR><BR>Amber-bearing and silver ores submit just as readily. Even the infused stone of the six elements is ground into pure shards — three from every block, more than a Fortune pick will knock loose.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUM_ORE_MACERATION.2=The taint deserves a special note: the extractor squeezes a springy polymer mass out of taint slime and tendrils, indistinguishable from rubber. Corruption made useful — there is something deeply wrong about that. And profitable.

tc.research_name.ALUMENTUM_FUEL=Alumentum: Machine Fuel
tc.research_text.ALUMENTUM_FUEL=Magical coal in an iron firebox
unboundtech.research_page.ALUMENTUM_FUEL.1=The generator is omnivorous: it cares little whether coal burns in its firebox or a clot of pure flame. Alumentum burns long and steady — one piece lasts a good while, and the EU meter climbs noticeably faster.<BR><BR>Just do not let the buffer overflow: alumentum resents having nowhere to put its strength.
unboundtech.research_page.ALUMENTUM_FUEL.2=Measurements, for those who keep accounts.<BR><BR>One lump of alumentum runs a generator for eighty seconds and yields 16,000 EU — four times what coal gives, which is 4,000 and gone in twenty.<BR><BR>The generator puts out its 10 EU a tick whatever you feed it; alumentum wins on density, not on power. A chest of alumentum carries the same force as four chests of coal — that, and not speed, is why it is worth hauling instead.

# Phase 3a — converters
tc.research_name.VIS_TO_EU_GENERATOR=Thaumic Alternator
tc.research_text.VIS_TO_EU_GENERATOR=Vis into current
unboundtech.research_page.VIS_TO_EU_GENERATOR.1=The trick is not to convert Vis, but to let it fall. Set the alternator beside a node and it siphons a thread of Ignis or Potentia, lets the aura drop through a thaumic coil, and catches what escapes as honest current.<BR><BR>The node barely notices. A fifth of every aspect is left untouched — drain past that and the node begins to wither, and a withered node feeds no one. The machine simply refuses.
unboundtech.research_page.VIS_TO_EU_GENERATOR.2=Do not expect a power plant. A fat node yields its surplus quickly, then settles into the pace of its own regeneration — a trickle, forever. That trickle is the honest yield; everything before it was you spending savings.<BR><BR>And do not crowd them. Two alternators within sixteen blocks fight over the same resonance and both fall silent.
unboundtech.research_page.VIS_TO_EU_GENERATOR.3=The numbers, briefly. One vis is 2,000 EU. The machine takes no more than one vis per second: in bursts that is a hundred EU a tick, but a low-voltage line will carry off only 32 — the rest banks in a 20,000 EU buffer, ten vis deep.<BR><BR>It senses eight blocks in every direction and will not touch a node in a jar on principle. No aspect drops below a fifth of its capacity in the node: a fat node holding fifty Ignis has forty vis of surplus — eighty thousand EU in one sitting. After that you get only what the node manages to grow back.
tc.research_name.EU_TO_VIS_ENGINE=Aetheric Engine
tc.research_text.EU_TO_VIS_ENGINE=Current back into vis
unboundtech.research_page.EU_TO_VIS_ENGINE.1=Pushing power back uphill costs dearly: four units of current for every one the alternator ever paid out. The engine floods a depleted node with raw force and lets the aura reshape it into whichever aspect the node is missing.<BR><BR>Wasteful — and worth it. A node that would take an hour to mend itself refills in a minute.
unboundtech.research_page.EU_TO_VIS_ENGINE.2=Do not dream of a closed loop. Alternator into engine and back returns a quarter of what it took; the rest is gone as heat and noise. Nature will not be argued with here.<BR><BR>The engine refills a node only to that node's own capacity. Restoring lost capacity — reviving a truly fading node — is another study entirely.
unboundtech.research_page.EU_TO_VIS_ENGINE.3=The numbers. One vis costs 8,000 EU, no faster than one vis per second. The buffer holds 40,000 EU — five vis. Intake is a medium-voltage line, up to 128 EU a tick; do not feed it higher, the tier will not take it.<BR><BR>On a full MV supply that is roughly one vis every three seconds against the node's own thirty — ten times faster. The aspect is chosen at random from those the node lacks, and the node fills only to its own capacity: the engine cannot raise that ceiling.
tc.research_name.LORE_RESONANCE_LIMITS=Resonance Limits
tc.research_text.LORE_RESONANCE_LIMITS=Why machines will not stand together
unboundtech.research_page.LORE_RESONANCE_LIMITS.1=Two alternators near one node do not share it — they beat against each other. Each coil twists the aura the other is trying to read, and the pair produce nothing but a faint whine and a headache.<BR><BR>Sixteen blocks is the measured distance at which the interference dies away. Not a law of nature so much as an engineering scar: every thaumaturge who ignored it has rebuilt their workshop wider.

tc.research_name.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES=Thaumodynamic Tables
tc.research_text.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES=Rates, throughputs and prices
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES.1=Set down in a column so nobody has to work it out twice.<BR><BR>Vis into current: 2,000 EU per unit.<BR>Current into vis: 8,000 EU per unit.<BR>Current into wand vis: 10,000 EU (still out of reach).<BR><BR>From which follows the important, unwelcome part: the loop node into current and back returns exactly a quarter of what went in. Three quarters leave as heat, hum and the smell of scorched insulation. A perpetual engine is impossible not because it is forbidden, but because the arithmetic refuses — and no amount of tuning mends that, only breaks it.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES.2=The same again, in units an engineer already trusts.<BR><BR>Coal in a generator: 4,000 EU.<BR>Alumentum: 16,000 EU — exactly four coal.<BR>One vis from a node: 2,000 EU — half a coal.<BR>A nitor block at a Stirling: 10 EU a tick, forever and free.<BR><BR>Put plainly, a lump of alumentum is worth eight vis. Eight vis out of a node means four minutes of waiting, while the alumentum burns through in eighty seconds — and it had to be brewed from something in the first place.<BR><BR>Accept the conclusion early: magic is not cheaper than machinery here. It simply never runs out.
unboundtech.research_page.THAUMODYNAMIC_TABLES.3=And throughput, which is what actually decides things.<BR><BR>Thaumic Alternator: up to 100 EU/t in bursts, 32 out on LV.<BR>The same over the long run, once the surplus is gone:<BR>— ordinary node: 3 EU/t (a vis every half minute);<BR>— bright node: 5 EU/t;<BR>— pale node: 2 EU/t;<BR>— fading node: nothing, and nothing ever after.<BR><BR>Aetheric Engine: 128 EU/t intake, a vis every three seconds.<BR><BR>Three units of current a tick looks absurd beside a bank of solar panels. Then again, panels stop at dusk, while a node shines on unattended and asks for no fuel. Do not build on one node — build on many, one alternator each, no closer than sixteen blocks apart.

tc.research_name.NITOR_HEAT=Nitor: Eternal Warmth
tc.research_text.NITOR_HEAT=A flame that needs no fuel
unboundtech.research_page.NITOR_HEAT.1=The Stirling engine wants heat, and nitor has nothing else to offer. Set a captured flame flush against the engine's intake and it yields a steady trickle of warmth — a modest twenty heat units a tick, but it has never once asked for fuel.<BR><BR>The engineers' boilers roar and gulp coal; the nitor simply burns, patient as a candle in a shrine. Weak, eternal, and quietly smug about both.
unboundtech.research_page.NITOR_HEAT.2=The sum is simple: a Stirling turns two units of heat into one of current, so nitor comes out at 10 EU a tick. Precisely what a coal generator manages — except that coal runs out and nitor does not.<BR><BR>Over a single day it gathers 240,000 EU, and all it asks is to be placed once. Want more? Place more candles: unlike alternators at nodes, they do not interfere with one another.

# Module asp — Advanced Solar Panels detected
tc.research_name.SOLAR_SUNNARIUM=Sunnarium: The Captured Sun
tc.research_text.SOLAR_SUNNARIUM=Solar alchemy of the engineers
unboundtech.research_page.SOLAR_SUNNARIUM.1=The engineers have done what alchemists only dreamed of: locked sunlight inside metal. Sunnarium warms the palm and refuses to dim even in a windowless cellar. The thaumometer is unambiguous — pure Lux bound with Potentia.<BR><BR>Which means the crucible will take it. Drop sunnarium into the melt when a recipe hungers for light or power: a costly source, but the sun never runs dry.
unboundtech.research_page.SOLAR_SUNNARIUM.2=Their solar panels deserve study as well — each tier hums with more trapped daylight than the last, and the quantum ones read almost like something not of this world.<BR><BR>A note for the future: once the converters are built, sunnarium promises a gentler path to iridium wand components. The workshop journal has a page reserved.

# Module mets — More Electric Tools detected
tc.research_name.TECHNO_MATERIALS=Titanium, Niobium and Living Metal
tc.research_text.TECHNO_MATERIALS=New metals under the thaumometer
unboundtech.research_page.TECHNO_MATERIALS.1=The toolmakers' workshop has delivered a whole shelf of new materials: stubborn titanium, cold-drinking niobium, superconducting weaves, circuits dense as honeycomb. The thaumometer reads them eagerly — Metallum in abundance, Potentia in the alloys, strict Ordo in the lattices.<BR><BR>Study them all: an aspect gathered from a scan is an aspect you need not hunt elsewhere.
unboundtech.research_page.TECHNO_MATERIALS.2=One find stands apart: nano living metal. The instrument shows Victus — life — inside a factory-made alloy. Either the engineers have crossed a line, or the border between golem craft and machinery was never as solid as the books claim.<BR><BR>Their laser weapons, meanwhile, hint at what a Tesla focus might one day become.

