tc.research_name.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM=Tempered Thaumium
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM=A metal that holds both current and magic
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM.1=Tempering is always a loss.<BR><BR>The blast furnace holds thaumium at a heat no crucible ever reached, under a pressure that rebuilds its lattice. Strength arrives, but part of the Praecantatio burns away: what was an even lilac glow sets into rare violet flecks in a grey body.<BR><BR>Alchemists call it spoilage, engineers call it stabilisation. Both are right: no wand will ever come of this metal, but it holds a machine casing without buckling under current.
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_THAUMIUM.2=There are two roads, and they come from opposite sides.<BR><BR>The magical road: take a finished thaumium ingot and send it into the blast furnace. Five minutes and a tankful of compressed air - exactly what the furnace asks for ordinary steel, not a tick longer.<BR><BR>The technical road: take a steel ingot and drop it into the crucible. Steel is already dead; magic is not poured into it but fused in - hence the stabilisers. Permutatio rebuilds the lattice, Vitreus holds the shape as it cools. The recipe is on the next page.<BR><BR>The ingot is the same either way, and both stack together. Take whichever road runs through the half of your workshop that already exists.

tc.research_name.TEMPERED_ARMOR=Forged Plate
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_ARMOR=Armour you hammer, not armour you infuse
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_ARMOR.1=Fortress armour is forged on an altar, and the vis lays each plate where it belongs. Tempered thaumium allows no such thing: its shape was set by the furnace, not by will. The smith swings a hammer where a thaumaturge would merely wish - and you can see it the moment you pick the suit up. It is crude. The plates are thicker, the seams show, the runes ran crooked and hold vis worse than they might have.<BR><BR>On the other hand, you can wear it the day after your furnace starts.<BR><BR>Count it honestly. Twenty-four armour across the set against diamond's twenty, and that is the only line you win. Toughness is halved, the enchanting table offers short dull lists, and every casting in this suit costs a fifth more. This is not a better diamond suit. It is a different one.

tc.research_name.TEMPERED_TOOLS=Tempered Thaumium Tools
tc.research_text.TEMPERED_TOOLS=A workhorse, not a wand
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_TOOLS.1="Tempered thaumium remembers both the anvil and the crucible. A tool made of it does not cut stone - it persuades it: no sparks, no shriek, just the stubbornness of metal that has already survived a blast furnace. Mages complain there is little magic left in it. It will outlive three of their wands."
unboundtech.research_page.TEMPERED_TOOLS.2=Where it stands in the line, by the numbers.<BR><BR>Thaumium tools: 500 durability, enchantability 18, the occasional favour from the metal.<BR>Void: 600 durability, enchantability 20, mends itself.<BR>Diamond: 1561 durability, enchantability 10.<BR>Ours: 1873 durability, enchantability 10, no favours at all.<BR><BR>So it outlasts everything a thaumaturge owns, and beats diamond by a fifth. It was paid for with research, a long haul of metalwork and a ceiling on enchanting: no amount of books will wring a good pickaxe out of it. There are no special effects and there will not be - that ground belongs to the order's own tools.

tc.research_name.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING=Electric Scribing Tools
tc.research_text.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING=A pen that writes with a discharge
unboundtech.research_page.ELECTRIC_SCRIBING.1=A pen that writes not with ink but with a discharge: the needle leaves a thin scorched line on the parchment, and such notes outlast handwritten ones. A charge does not last long - but set it on a charging pad, go make tea, and you can write again. Engineers find this obvious; thaumaturges find it humiliating.<BR><BR>Four thousand EU, fifty per research point, eighty points on a full charge. An ordinary inkwell wins on capacity; this one wins because the capacity comes back for free.<BR><BR>Carry only one kind at a time: the table grabs the first inkwell it finds, not the best one.


